  <p>
    Most pages on this site start from a receptor and work outward to what it does.
    This one starts from a disease and works back to why the melanocortin system
    turned out to be the answer. <strong>Erythropoietic protoporphyria</strong> is a
    rare inherited disorder of how the body builds <strong>heme</strong> — the
    iron-centred molecule that carries oxygen in blood and sits at the heart of
    countless enzymes. It is also the first condition for which the melanocortin
    pigment switch, <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>, was deliberately turned into
    an approved drug.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>A pathway that stops one step short</h2>
  <p>
    Heme is assembled in eight enzymatic steps. The very last one is carried out by an
    enzyme called <strong>ferrochelatase</strong>, whose single job is to insert an iron
    atom into the finished ring — a molecule called <strong>protoporphyrin IX</strong>
    (PPIX) — to complete heme. In EPP, ferrochelatase activity is reduced, typically to
    somewhere around a third of normal. The pathway runs right up to PPIX and then
    stalls, so PPIX accumulates in red blood cell precursors, spills into the plasma,
    and reaches the skin.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The genetics are quietly interesting. Most patients are not simple recessives: they
    carry a severe loss-of-function mutation in the ferrochelatase gene on one
    chromosome <em>and</em>, on the other, a common low-expression variant (the
    “IVS3-48C” allele) that much of the population also carries harmlessly. Only when a
    damaging mutation lands opposite that weak allele does total activity fall far enough
    for disease — which is why EPP can appear to move through a family in a way that
    looks neither cleanly dominant nor cleanly recessive.<Cite n={1} /> A small minority
    have a closely related disease, X-linked protoporphyria, where the problem is an
    overactive upstream enzyme making too much PPIX rather than a failure to clear it —
    same molecule, same light reaction, different starting point.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>Why light becomes pain</h2>
  <p>
    PPIX is not an inert waste product. It is a <strong>photosensitiser</strong>: a
    molecule built to absorb light. Its strongest absorption sits in the violet-blue
    band around <strong>400–410 nm</strong> — visible light, not ultraviolet. When
    sunlight reaches PPIX sitting in the small blood vessels of the skin, the molecule
    is kicked into an excited state and passes that energy to nearby oxygen, generating
    <strong>reactive oxygen species</strong> that damage the vessel walls and surrounding
    tissue. The result is not a slow burn but an immediate, stinging, burning pain, often
    with swelling and redness, within minutes of exposure.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the detail that sets EPP apart from ordinary sun sensitivity, and the reason
    it is so often missed for years. Because the trigger is visible light rather than UV,
    it passes through ordinary window glass and through cloud; sunscreen aimed at UV does
    little; and the reaction is pain in real time rather than a sunburn that shows up
    hours later. Children with EPP learn to fear sunny windows and car rides, and the
    diagnosis is frequently delayed because the skin may show no blister to point to —
    only a child in agony with, to an onlooker, nothing visibly wrong.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

