  <h2>The cost beyond the skin</h2>
  <p>
    EPP is dominated by the skin, but it is not only a skin disease. The excess PPIX is
    cleared by the liver into bile, and that load can form gallstones and, in a small but
    serious fraction of patients, overwhelm the liver and threaten protoporphyric liver
    failure. Many patients are also mildly anaemic. None of this changes the day-to-day
    reality — a life organised around avoiding light — but it is why EPP is monitored as
    a whole-body condition and not treated as a cosmetic nuisance.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>Where the melanocortin system comes in</h2>
  <p>
    For most of its history EPP had no treatment at all, only avoidance: heavy clothing,
    shade, and a life lived around the sun. The insight that changed things did not come
    from fixing the enzyme. It came from the other end — from the skin’s own defence.
    The pigment <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> normally drives, eumelanin, happens to be
    an excellent absorber of exactly the visible light that excites PPIX. Build up more of
    it, and you put a shield between the light and the culprit molecule.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is precisely what <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide (Scenesse)</a> does. It
    is a durable copy of α-MSH that switches on MC1R to lay down protective eumelanin
    <em>independent of sunlight</em> — so a person who cannot safely tan the normal way can
    still accumulate the pigment that buys them time in the light. In the pivotal trials,
    patients given the implant spent significantly more pain-free time in sunlight than
    those given placebo.<Cite n={2} /> On the strength of that, the FDA approved it on
    <strong>8 October 2019</strong>, the first therapy ever cleared for EPP.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The approach is now being pushed from implant toward pill. <a href="/dersimelagon">
    Dersimelagon</a>, an oral small-molecule MC1R agonist, drives the same pigment response
    by mouth; a Phase 2 trial reported in 2023 showed it lengthened the time EPP patients
    could tolerate sunlight before symptoms began.<Cite n={4} /> Both drugs share the same
    logic and the same honest limit: they treat the light reaction, not the enzyme. PPIX
    still accumulates; the liver risk is unchanged; what improves is how much daylight a
    person can bear.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    EPP is a clean illustration of the theme this whole site runs on — one economical
    system, reused far from where you would expect. A defect in heme chemistry, with
    nothing obviously to do with pigment, turned out to be best managed by borrowing the
    melanocortin pigment switch and running it on purpose. It is not a cure, and the
    field is still working toward one that addresses the protoporphyrin itself. But it is
    a real answer to a disease that had none, and it is where MC1R pharmacology first
    proved it could leave the laboratory. For the drug, see{' '}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>; for the oral successor, see{' '}
    <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a>; for the receptor they both act on, see{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R — the pigment switch</a>.
  </p>

